# Audio Token Minter - Project Intelligence

## Project Patterns

### Component Structure
- Each component is in its own file in the `src/components` directory
- Components follow a functional approach using React hooks
- Components are exported as default exports

### State Management
- Local component state is managed using React's `useState` hook
- Props are used to pass data between components
- Callbacks are used for child-to-parent communication

### Styling
- CSS is organized in the `index.css` file
- Component-specific styles use class names that match the component name
- CSS variables are used for colors and other theme values

### Error Handling
- Try-catch blocks are used for error handling
- Errors are logged to the console
- User-friendly error messages are displayed in the UI

### Testing
- Development mode is used for testing without a real wallet
- Simulated functionality is used for features that require external services

## Code Conventions

### Naming
- Component files use PascalCase (e.g., `AudioRecorder.tsx`)
- Component functions use PascalCase (e.g., `function AudioRecorder()`)
- Variables and functions use camelCase (e.g., `handleRecordingComplete`)
- CSS classes use kebab-case (e.g., `audio-recorder`)

### TypeScript
- TypeScript is used throughout the project
- Props are defined using interfaces
- Function parameters and return types are explicitly typed

### React Patterns
- Functional components are used instead of class components
- React hooks are used for state and side effects
- Components are kept small and focused on a single responsibility

## Development Workflow

### Local Development
- Run `npm run dev` to start the development server
- Access the app at `http://localhost:5173`
- Use the "Simulate Connected" button to test without a real wallet

### Building for Production
- Run `npm run build` to create a production build
- The build output is in the `dist` directory
- Deploy the contents of the `dist` directory to a hosting provider

## Project Structure

### Key Files and Directories
- `src/components/AudioRecorder.tsx`: Audio recording functionality
- `src/components/AudioTokenMinter.tsx`: Token minting functionality
- `src/App.tsx`: Main application component
- `src/index.css`: Styles for the application
- `src/wagmi.ts`: Wagmi configuration for wallet connections

### Memory Bank
- `memory-bank/projectbrief.md`: Project overview and requirements
- `memory-bank/techContext.md`: Technical details and implementation
- `memory-bank/systemPatterns.md`: Architecture and design patterns
- `memory-bank/productContext.md`: User experience and product goals
- `memory-bank/progress.md`: Current status and next steps
- `memory-bank/activeContext.md`: Current focus and recent changes

## Known Issues and Workarounds

### Audio Recording in Browser
- Audio recording requires user permission
- Some browsers may not support the MediaRecorder API
- Testing in a secure context (HTTPS or localhost) is required

### Wallet Connection
- Wallet connection requires a compatible wallet provider
- In development mode, use the "Simulate Connected" button for testing
- The Farcaster Frame SDK requires a Farcaster client to work properly

### Token Minting
- Token minting is currently simulated
- A real NFT contract address is required for actual minting
- Gas fees will be required for actual minting

## Future Development Guidelines

### Adding New Features
- Keep components small and focused
- Follow the existing patterns for state management and error handling
- Update documentation when adding new features

### Integrating External Services
- Use environment variables for API keys and other sensitive information
- Add proper error handling for API calls
- Consider fallback options for when services are unavailable

### Deployment
- Build the app using `npm run build`
- Deploy the contents of the `dist` directory
- Register the app with Farcaster for production use
